-- The DATE-LITERAL exhibit for the DMN exporter (spec §15.4).
--
-- `daydate.l4`:79-100 recommends `YMD y m d` over `Date d m y` for new code,
-- on two grounds: ISO argument order makes a day/month transposition harder to
-- WRITE, and `YMD` REFUSES an out-of-range component where `Date` silently
-- rolls it. For a while the DMN exporter punished exactly that: a module
-- written the recommended way emitted raw L4 (`YMD OF 1983, 1, 1`) that no
-- engine can parse, where the same day spelled `Date 1 1 1983` emitted
-- `date("1983-01-01")`. This module is the regression pin for the repair.
--
-- Deliberately one of each POSITION a folded date literal can occupy, because
-- they are four different code paths off the one recogniser:
--
--   * `commencement` / `the appointed day` / `the transition day`
--        a nullary decision whose BODY is a `YMD` literal -- the boxed literal
--        expression, i.e. `renderFeelIn`;
--   * `the regime that applies`
--        the same three as decision-table CELL endpoints, reached by the
--        named-constant hop through `tcDateConstants` -- i.e. `constantOf`
--        never sees a `YMD` node at all, it sees the folded `Day`, which is
--        why populating that map is part of the fix;
--   * `the filing fee`
--        a law-time chain (§15) whose arms are `YMD`-dated: one arm naming a
--        regime constant and one written as a BARE `YMD` literal, because
--        those are the two things an inline right-hand side may be and they
--        reach different code (`constantDay`'s one hop, then the literal
--        fold). Both must land in a single-column `hitPolicy="UNIQUE"` table
--        over `RULES_EFFECTIVE_DATE` with half-open interval cells;
--   * `the anniversary`
--        the NEGATIVE control, below.
--
-- `the appointed day` is 2024-02-29 on purpose. A leap day is the cheapest
-- proof that the fold consults a calendar rather than range-checking three
-- numbers, and it is the boundary the cases straddle in `ymd-dates.cases.json`.
--
-- THE NEGATIVE CONTROL LIVES NEXT DOOR, in `not-ok/ymd-unfoldable-date.l4`,
-- and not in this file, for a mechanical reason worth stating: a raw-L4
-- <literalExpression> body makes zeebe-dmn refuse to parse the WHOLE file
-- (MEASURED 2026-08-02 on a copy of this exhibit carrying a computed `YMD`:
-- "PARSE  INVALID: FEEL expression: failed to parse expression
-- 'YMD OF (1983 PLUS 40), 1, 1'"), so a module that both ships an unfoldable
-- date and runs on both engines cannot exist. The three refusals this
-- exhibit's repair must NOT have widened -- a computed component, a component
-- triple that is not a real calendar day, and a day/month transposition -- are
-- asserted there, and in `jl4/tests/DmnExport.hs`.
--
-- The heading below is load-bearing: `l4 export --to=dmn` with no
-- `--model-name` takes the model name from the outermost `§`.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

§ `YMD date literals`

-- Required by the rule-version axis: an unpinned `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE` read
-- resolves to the localized current day, and localization needs a timezone.
TIMEZONE IS "Asia/Singapore"

@ref British Nationality Act 1981, s 53(2) — commencement
GIVETH A DATE
`commencement` MEANS YMD 1983 1 1

-- A leap day, deliberately: `YMD 2024 2 29` is a real date and must fold,
-- while `YMD 2023 2 29` is not one and must not (see `not-ok/`).
GIVETH A DATE
`the appointed day` MEANS YMD 2024 2 29

GIVETH A DATE
`the transition day` MEANS YMD 2026 1 1

-- The date the CASE is about, as opposed to the date the LAW is about. It is
-- an ordinary input, so the comparisons below are ordinary decision-table
-- cells rather than law-time intervals.
ASSUME `the application date` IS A DATE

GIVETH A STRING
`the regime that applies` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the application date` AT LEAST `the transition day` THEN "transitional"
           IF `the application date` AT LEAST `the appointed day`  THEN "appointed"
           IF `the application date` AT LEAST `commencement`       THEN "original"
           OTHERWISE "pre-commencement"

-- Before commencement there is no fee to answer for, so a rule date earlier
-- than 1983-01-01 is a curated refusal rather than an answer. Same device, and
-- the same reasoning, as `gst-rate.l4`'s and the Reg CF corpus's ASSUME floor.
ASSUME `no fee is prescribed before commencement on 1983-01-01` IS A NUMBER

GIVETH A NUMBER
`the filing fee` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE` AT LEAST `the transition day` THEN 1000
           IF `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE` AT LEAST YMD 1983 1 1         THEN 250
           OTHERWISE `no fee is prescribed before commencement on 1983-01-01`

-- A date driving a number driving a number, so the chain is shown end to end
-- in a real engine rather than only in the XML.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the fee with surcharge` MEANS `the filing fee` TIMES 2
